# Windows batch files must keep CRLF line endings on checkout, on every
# platform. cmd.exe reads a .cmd in the OEM codepage and mis-parses bare LF:
# the `rem` lines stop being comments and it tries to run them as commands.
# Without this, a clone on Linux/macOS — or a repo with core.autocrlf=input —
# stages LF, windows/build.ps1 copies that straight into the installer, and
# every `freeclaw` invocation prints a screenful of "is not recognized" before
# it works.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# PowerShell scripts keep CRLF too. install.ps1 and uninstall.ps1 are also
# deliberately pure ASCII: they are fetched and run with `irm ... | iex`, which
# decodes the raw bytes, and a BOM or a stray non-ASCII character in that path
# is a parse error rather than a rendering glitch.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# The Inno Setup script needs its UTF-8 BOM preserved — without one the
# compiler reads the file as ANSI and the em dashes in the wizard's own text
# render as mojibake. Treating it as binary keeps git from touching either the
# BOM or the line endings.
*.iss -text

# Shell scripts must keep LF, including when checked out on Windows: the
# install/update scripts are run by bash on Linux and macOS, which chokes on
# a CRLF shebang.
*.sh text eol=lf
